  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a mixed picture: Port Washington ran lean on capacity and saw a real productivity dip, while Hicksville got a strong output from minimal staffing but faced a scheduling cliff this week.\n\n**Port Washington**: You're down 10% YoY ($2,920 vs $3,246), but the culprit is staffing, not performance. You had six groomers producing $406 per groomer\u2014well below your $480 trailing-30-day baseline. Core groom revenue fell $150 week-over-week, and retail dropped 20%, which suggests some friction in front-of-house execution or customer traffic timing. The good news: MTD you're running 8.5% ahead of DOW-adjusted expectation ($53,663 actual vs $49,446 expected), and YTD you're up 45% on appointments. The lapse pool here represents $297K in annual value\u2014meaningful reactivation opportunity if you can free up groomer capacity to accommodate it.\n\n**Hicksville** is the surprise: Julia B. alone generated $500/groomer, 19% above your $420 baseline, yet total revenue fell 15% because you only had *one* groomer yesterday (down from three last Monday). This week you've only booked four appointments versus eight last week\u2014a 50% drop. That's not Julia's performance; that's a booking or demand issue. Retail was strong (+33%), but you can't build a location on retail margin alone. MTD you're still +16.2% versus DOW-adjusted expectation, so the underlying business is solid.\n\n**One thing to own**: Port Washington's YTD 45% growth is exceptional and should anchor your confidence in the core model.\n\n**One thing to watch**: Hicksville's appointment pipeline for this week is anemic (4 booked, 3 groomers scheduled). Confirm tomorrow's and next week's booking cadence immediately.\n\n**Capacity & coverage**: Today: Port Washington 7 groomers / 0h bathers; Hicksville 3 groomers / 0h bathers. Tomorrow: Port Washington 7 groomers / 7h bathers; Hicksville 3 groomers / 0h bathers. Next 7 days: Port Washington 38 groomer-days / 43h bathers; Hicksville 18 groomer-days / 0h bathers."
